{#- Life & Interests: the Bet Tracker feature section (#bet-tracker). Copy lives here;
    the project card/page text lives in personal-projects-data.js (id "bet-tracker"). -#}
<section class="band bt-band" id="bet-tracker" aria-labelledby="bt-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    {{ ui.sectionLabel("L-315", "Bet tracker") }}
    <h2 id="bt-title">A bet tracker that <em>keeps score for us.</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">My friends and I keep a running slate of season-long bets: player props, team win totals, awards, futures and parlays. I got tired of checking box scores and doing the math by hand, so I built a tracker that pulls the stats from ESPN on its own and shows exactly where every ticket stands, on any phone or computer.</p>

    <div class="bt-layout">
      <figure class="bt-shot">
        {% picture "images/personal/betting/bet-tracker-dashboard.png", "Bet Tracker dashboard with summary cards for tickets, total staked, potential payout, record and closing-line value, above a stake versus payout chart grouped by bet type", "gallery", { sizes: "(max-width: 820px) 92vw, 780px" } %}
        <figcaption>The dashboard: ticket count, money at stake, potential payout, record and CLV up top, then exposure by bet type.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="bt-shot bt-shot-phone">
        {% picture "images/personal/betting/bet-tracker-mobile.png", "Bet Tracker on a phone, showing the header actions and summary cards in a single column", "card", { sizes: "(max-width: 820px) 60vw, 240px" } %}
        <figcaption>Built for checking scores from the couch, too.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <ul class="bt-points">
      <li>
        <h3>ESPN auto-refresh</h3>
        <p>Every hour a GitHub Action pulls each player's season stats and every team's record from ESPN, then commits the update. Nobody has to type in a yardage total, and the Refresh ESPN button gives an instant check between runs.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Charts and pace scoring</h3>
        <p>Progress bars show how far each bet has come, and the Chart Report scores <em>pace</em>. A receiver right on track for his line three weeks in only has 18% of his yards, but he scores about two-thirds, not 18%. Win totals compare a team's win rate with the rate the line needs, and the overall number is weighted by payout. A "?" button explains every formula.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Entry form and CLV</h3>
        <p>Bets get added, edited and settled from a form instead of hand-editing JSON. A save commits straight to the repo through the GitHub API, merged onto the newest data so the hourly refresh is never lost. Each ticket can store the odds I got and the closing odds, and the tracker reports my average closing-line value and how often I beat the close.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>The tech</h3>
        <p>It's a static site with no build step: plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript, with Chart.js for the charts, hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub Actions handles the refresh. Changes go to a staging copy first, and I promote them to the live site once they've been tested.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="bt-shot bt-shot-wide">
      {% picture "images/personal/betting/bet-tracker-chart-report.png", "Bet Tracker Chart Report showing eligible entries, allocated stake, implied payout value, a 53 percent overall progress bar, and pace-scored rows for team win totals and player props", "gallery", { sizes: "(max-width: 960px) 92vw, 900px" } %}
      <figcaption>The Chart Report: pace score for every prop, win total and parlay leg, with the math written out under each row.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="btn-row bt-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://zacherytaylor.github.io/bet-tracker/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the Bet Tracker</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/projects/bet-tracker/">Project page</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
